libopenwch — provenance and licensing
=====================================

This file records where the code and the ideas in this repository came from,
and under what terms.  It exists so that a downstream user can tell at a glance
which parts are original, which are derived, and which were consulted as
documentation only.

License summary
---------------

  Library code                LGPL-3.0-or-later     LICENSE
  (include/, lib/, ld/, mk/)                        COPYING.LGPL3
  scripts/checkpatch.pl       GPL-2.0               COPYING.GPL2
  Documentation and governance
  (project.md, phase.md,       CC0-1.0 / public domain
   status.md, AGENTS.md,       (original prose, no restriction intended)
   README.md, NOTICE)

`LICENSE` carries the GNU LGPL version 3 text and is the top-level entry point
for license scanners.  `COPYING.LGPL3` is the same text under its libopencm3
name.  Because the LGPL version 3 is defined as the GPL version 3 plus
additional permissions, `COPYING.GPL3` is also shipped: it is the text the LGPL
incorporates by reference.  `COPYING.GPL2` exists solely for
`scripts/checkpatch.pl`, which is GPL-2.0-only and cannot be relicensed.

Applications that merely link against libopenwch are unaffected by the LGPL;
the same is true of libopencm3, from which this split is inherited.

Derived from libopencm3 (LGPL-3.0-or-later)
-------------------------------------------

The build system, the directory conventions, parts of the linker script, and
several scripts are derived from libopencm3 and remain under the LGPL:

  Makefile                     structure: TARGETS recursion, failure stamps
  lib/Makefile.include         archive rules
  mk/gcc-config.mk             toolchain variable module
  mk/gcc-rules.mk              %.elf/%.bin/%.hex/%.o rules
  mk/genlink-config.mk         device -> library selection
  mk/genlink-rules.mk          generated linker script rule
  scripts/genlink.py           device database query tool
  scripts/irq2nvic_h           IRQ header generator (heavily reworked)
  scripts/genlinktest.sh       device database test harness
  ld/linker.ld.S               generic linker script skeleton
  ld/devices.data              device database format
  include/libopencm3/**        header conventions, Doxygen style, MMIO macros
  HACKING, HACKING_COMMON_DOC  coding rules and the new-family checklist
  COPYING.LGPL3, COPYING.GPL3  taken verbatim
  LICENSE                      LGPL-3.0 text, same as COPYING.LGPL3
  COPYING.GPL2                 GNU GPL version 2, for scripts/checkpatch.pl

The individual copyright notices in those files are retained.

Consulted as documentation only — no code copied
------------------------------------------------

WCH's official EVT packages for the CH32V003 and the CH583 were read to
establish register addresses, bit positions, field layouts, and the ordering of
clock and flash sequences.  Register addresses and bit assignments are
interface facts and are not copyrightable expression, but the EVT *code* is
not, and none of it was copied: every function in this library is written
against the reference manual's register descriptions and is deliberately named
differently (the EVT uses CamelCase; this library does not).

  ch32v003-main/EVT/EXAM/SRC/Peripheral/inc/ch32v00x*.h
  ch32v003-main/EVT/EXAM/SRC/Core/core_riscv.h
  ch32v003-main/EVT/EXAM/SRC/Startup/startup_ch32v00x.S
  ch32v003-main/EVT/EXAM/SRC/Ld/Link.ld
  ch583-main/EVT/EXAM/SRC/StdPeriphDriver/inc/CH583SFR.h
  ch583-main/EVT/EXAM/SRC/StdPeriphDriver/CH58x_*.c, inc/CH58x_*.h
  ch583-main/EVT/EXAM/SRC/Startup/startup_CH583.S
  ch583-main/EVT/EXAM/SRC/Ld/Link.ld

The QingKe (WCH RISC-V core) vector table format, the PFIC register layout, the
`mtvec = base | 3` mode and the RWA safe-access protocol were all verified
against those files.

Influenced the toolchain strategy
---------------------------------

ch32fun is MIT licensed.  Its approach to the generic `riscv64-unknown-elf`
toolchain shaped the toolchain probe chain in mk/gcc-config.mk and the
`-nostdlib` / `-ffunction-sections` recipe used by the application template.
No ch32fun source was copied.  The bundled `misc/libgcc.a` from ch32fun is
NOT used: the Debian riscv64-unknown-elf toolchain ships a working
`rv32e/ilp32e` libgcc, which the build probes for.

  ch32fun-master/ch32fun/ch32fun.mk
  ch32fun-master/ch32fun/ch32fun.ld
  ch32fun-master/ch32fun/ch32fun.c
  ch32fun-master/ch32fun/ch5xxhw.h
  ch32fun-master/examples/template/
  ch32fun-master/misc/CH32V003xx.svd

The CH32V003 SVD file was used to resolve the AFIO remap bit layout and the
DBGMCU question, where WCH's EVT and ch32fun's headers disagree with each
other.

Original work
-------------

Everything else is original to this project, in particular:

  include/libopenwch/qingke/**       the RISC-V core layer
  lib/qingke/**                      reset path, PFIC, SysTick
  include/libopenwch/ch32v0/**       CH32V00x register maps and drivers
  include/libopenwch/ch5xx58x/**     CH58x register maps and drivers
  lib/ch32v0/**, lib/ch5xx58x/**     the driver implementations
  lib/mini_libc/**                   the freestanding mini libc
  include/libopenwch/mini_libc.h     its declarations
  template/**                        the application skeleton
  tests/**                           the API smoke tests

The mini libc is an independent implementation of the standard string and
memory routines, written for this project from their published semantics.  It
is not derived from newlib, from picolibc, or from any other C library, and it
carries the same LGPL-3.0-or-later terms as the rest of the library.
